Found while measuring karr #150. It is what makes that hole reachable through an ordinary .sops.yaml, with no hand-edited metadata anywhere.

unencrypted_regex and encrypted_regex are matched here by Perl and by Go with RE2, and the two disagree on every character class whose meaning depends on Unicode. RE2 is ASCII-only for \w, \d, \s and the POSIX classes; Perl is Unicode-aware for any string carrying the UTF-8 flag -- which is every non-ASCII key our parsers produce.

Measured end to end, sops 3.13.3, one .sops.yaml, sops -e, then File::SOPS->rotate. No hand editing at any point:

  unencrypted_regex    key         sops        we store
  ^\w+$                cafe'       encrypts    bare
  ^n\d$                n + U+0663  encrypts    bare
  ^[[:alpha:]]+$       cafe'       encrypts    bare
  ^a\sb$               a + NBSP+b  encrypts    bare
  ^\w+$                U+5BC6      encrypts    bare

Before karr #150 every one of those rotates wrote the secret to disk in plaintext at exit 0. They are refused now, so the SYMPTOM is closed and this is what is left: a rule that classifies a key differently from the implementation that wrote the file. In the other direction (encrypted_regex) the same divergence encrypts a value sops leaves readable, which is not refused today either.

Note the second half of the trap: whether Perl uses Unicode or ASCII semantics for \w depends on the internal UTF-8 flag of the key, so the SAME key decides differently depending on where the string came from -- "caf\x{e9}" written in Perl source is latin-1 and matches ASCII-only, the same key out of YAML::XS carries the flag and matches Unicode-aware. Reading that flag is exactly what ADR 0003 forbids everywhere else in this distribution; here the regex engine reads it for us.

Likely answer to measure: /a (ASCII-only) on the two rule matches, which is RE2 semantics for these classes, plus a look at what else RE2 and Perl disagree on for a pattern sops accepts. _re2_incompatible_construct in SOPS.pm already knows the other half of this problem for creation-rule path_regex.

